{%- comment -%}
  Progress towards free delivery.

  Shared by the cart drawer and the cart page so the rule, the wording and the
  maths exist once. All of it is computed in Liquid from the real cart —
  nothing here is passed in from a script.

  It counts pairs, not money. The promise this site makes everywhere — the
  announcement bar, the shipping policy, the buy widget's tiers — is "free UK
  delivery on two pairs or more". Measuring that against a money threshold said
  something different and wrong: with one pair in the basket the bar read
  "£15.00 away from free delivery", which is neither the price of the second
  pair (£16.99) nor anything a shopper could act on. It now says what to do —
  add one more pair — and what it saves, which is the £4.99 delivery charge.

  Renders nothing when the rule is switched off, which is how a merchant turns
  the promise off: an unset minimum should not become "0 more to go".
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign min_items = settings.free_delivery_min_items | plus: 0
  assign charge = settings.delivery_charge_pence | plus: 0

  assign show = false
  if settings.cart_show_progress and min_items > 0 and cart.item_count > 0
    assign show = true
  endif

  assign short_by = min_items | minus: cart.item_count
  assign qualified = false
  if short_by <= 0
    assign qualified = true
  endif

  assign pct = cart.item_count | times: 100 | divided_by: min_items
  if pct > 100
    assign pct = 100
  endif
-%}

{%- if show -%}
  <div class="hs-cartd__progress">
    <p class="hs-caption hs-cartd__progress-label">
      {%- if qualified -%}
        {% render 'hs-icon', icon: 'check', size: 14 %}
        {{ 'cart.delivery.qualified' | t }}
      {%- else -%}
        {%- comment -%}
          One string for one more pair and another for several, so the line
          reads as English either way rather than "add 1 more pairs".
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- if charge > 0 -%}
          {%- comment -%}
            The charge is formatted on its own line first. Piped at the end of
            the `t` call, `money` ran on the whole translated sentence — and
            money of a string is £0.00, which is exactly what the bar showed.
            Same left-to-right filter trap as the WhatsApp link.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          {%- assign charge_money = charge | money -%}
          {{ 'cart.delivery.add_more_saving' | t: count: short_by, amount: charge_money }}
        {%- else -%}
          {{ 'cart.delivery.add_more' | t: count: short_by }}
        {%- endif -%}
      {%- endif -%}
    </p>
    {%- comment -%}
      A progressbar, so the value is the accessible value rather than decoration.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <div
      class="hs-cartd__bar{% if qualified %} is-qualified{% endif %}"
      role="progressbar"
      aria-valuemin="0"
      aria-valuemax="100"
      aria-valuenow="{{ pct }}"
      aria-label="{{ 'cart.delivery.progress_label' | t }}"
    >
      <span class="hs-cartd__bar-fill" style="width: {{ pct }}%"></span>
    </div>
  </div>
{%- endif -%}
